A||N||15||Monkeyman||'86 2.8 in an '85??||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||05:29 PM||||Will a 2.8 from an '86-up fit in my '85 GT?  Are there any mods required?  What about a 2.8 from an '88?  Was the 2.8 in an '88 changed any?  Improved?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||12-16-1999||09:55 PM||||Monkey... The 88 had a different pan/front cover. (Maybe because of the revised suspension and redesigned cradle, maybe to fix an oil leak problem(?). Not sure, though.) Also, it became internally balanced. All balancing was done in the crankshaft, instead of the flywheel or flexplate. As far as I know, the 88 was the only different year. I suspect that they are still interchangeable.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-16-1999||10:12 PM||||The '88 also dropped the torque reaction strut thing. All it has are the 3 mounts on bottom(1 engine, 2 transmission) and the dogbone.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||02:31 AM||||So I can use any motor in my '85?||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-17-1999||06:45 PM||||Yes, including one from a Celebrity, GP, GA, Cavalier... That is the best thing about GM products.  They are very available, if you use your wits.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||11:34 PM||||OK, but I thought that I would have to remount the starter if I used a motor from something other than a Fiero (something about it getting in the way of the tranny?).  Not that I plan to go with anything but the stock 2.8 for now.<P>Called ACE today about $100 2.8s.  The ones for 100 bucks need to be rebuilt before being used.  He occasionally does have some that are relatively low mileage motors in good shape ready to drop in, but they are around $500 (still cheaper than a rebuild).||||reg||1
Z||000006||chris6878||12-18-1999||12:29 AM||||MonkeyMan...<P>I just recently rebuild my engine in my 85 SE (V6). I did it for a little under $400(US). I put a new crank, cam, cam bearings, rod and main bearings, had the rods reconditioned at a machine shop, new timing gears and chain, new lifters, and a gasket set.<P>But.. that is a good deal for the low mileage engines for $500...   and least u dont have to do the work rebuilding it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000007||chris6878||12-18-1999||12:31 AM||||oppss...  also new rings...         would be pointless to rebuild if u didnt replaced them..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||12-18-1999||04:40 AM||||85-87 V6 engines were all identical. For some reason, 85 was rated at 140 then the rest were rated at 135 hp. (?) As for the 88 it was a little different with a different flywheel and crank like was said before. Also some 87 and all 88 water pumps were put on with Torx bolts(T-15, T-40 and T-50 I think.) instead of 10 mm, 13 mm and 15 mm hex heads, (Ive done a few water pumps). Torx are a little more trickier to work with, if you strip out the "star" you are in for a fight to get that water pump off. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||12-18-1999||04:41 AM||||85-87 V6 engines were all identical. For some reason, 85 was rated at 140 then the rest were rated at 135 hp. (?) As for the 88 it was a little different with a different flywheel and crank like was said before. Also some 87 and all 88 water pumps were put on with Torx bolts(T-15, T-40 and T-50 I think.) instead of 10 mm, 13 mm and 15 mm hex heads, (Ive done a few water pumps). Torx are a little more trickier to work with, if you strip out the "star" you are in for a fight to get that water pump off. ||||reg||2
Z||000010||fiero56||12-18-1999||09:10 AM||||The '85 had 5 more horses due to lower emissions laws. In '86 and later emissions became stricter, resulting in a 5hp loss for the v-6.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||12-18-1999||06:22 PM||||Is the '88 a better motor?  All ACE said was that the plenum (?) was stamped/engraved as opposed to a sticker ("Fiero" or something?).  Will an '88 drop in an '85 or will something need to be changed?||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||12-18-1999||10:48 PM||||If you use an 88 motor, make sure you use the 88 flywheel too. They switched to a neutrally balanced flywheel in 88. You can't use the older flywheel with it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||12-18-1999||11:11 PM||||Mounts and everything will be the same, except '88 doesn't use the drive line absorber thing. '87 and '88 had the engaved plenums. Only real difference is in the balancing. Should be fine.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||12-19-1999||01:09 AM||||What a deal!  Thanx, guys.  Is the flywheel attached to the motor?  Do I understand correctly that the flywheel is the big, round plate with the teeth on it?  Goes in between the motor and tranny maybe?  Will the engraved plenum work on an earlier motor?  And what's the "drive line absorber thing"?  TIA||||reg||1
Z||000015||batboy||12-19-1999||10:46 AM||||Yes, the flywheel bolts to the "rear" of the engine between the tranny and the motor. The outer teeth is what the starter engages when you crank the switch. I believe the 88 engine uses a different front balancer too.||||reg||1
